WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
Safe error behavior separates user experience from operator visibility.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:16.680
Users should receive clear, bounded messages that do not expose stack traces, connection strings, internal paths, framework details, tokens, or raw database errors.

3
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:23.740
Operators need more diagnostic context, but that context belongs in protected logs and monitoring systems.

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.140
Security-relevant logging should be intentional.

5
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:44.200
Authentication events, authorization failures, administrative actions, account lifecycle changes, important data changes, suspicious validation failures, file upload decisions, configuration changes, and deployment events can all help teams detect and investigate problems.

6
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:51.020
Logs should be structured enough to search and correlate, but sanitized enough to avoid becoming a sensitive data store.

7
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.380
Correlation IDs and audit trails make incidents easier to understand.

8
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:02.280
A single request may touch middleware, application code, a database, an external service, and a background job.

9
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.940
Useful correlation lets an operator connect those events without guessing.

10
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:15.600
Audit records should preserve who did what, to which resource, under which context, and when, while respecting privacy and retention requirements.

11
00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.120
Dependencies are part of the application.

12
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:29.660
NuGet packages, transitive dependencies, .NET runtimes, base images, hosting components, build tools, and deployment actions influence what ships.

13
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:37.280
Teams should review dependency health, known vulnerabilities, maintenance status, license expectations, and update cadence.

14
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.820
A package that is convenient today still needs an owner tomorrow.

15
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.180
CI/CD systems deserve the same security attention as production applications.

16
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:56.960
Release credentials, package publishing tokens, signing keys, deployment identities, environment variables, build logs, and artifact stores can all become high-value targets.

17
00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:05.140
Use least privilege, controlled secrets, branch protections, review requirements, and environment-specific deployment policies.

18
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.140
Deployment security connects configuration, code, and operations.

19
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:20.160
Production monitoring, alerting, rollback readiness, incident response contacts, patch processes, and runtime maintenance keep the application secure after the release.

20
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:30.820
A secure ASP.NET Core application is not only one that passes review; it is one the team can safely operate, update, and investigate over time.
